{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_345","type":"reasoning","text":"Important: the B00 baseline validator reads each packet's `spec.path` (when state=existing) and requires its frontmatter `id` to be non-empty. S04's spec path in the inventory is `harness/specs/tasks/browser-effect-provenance-boundary.md` (state existing) — which exists. But my new spec is a different file. The S03 approach: the inventory's S03 spec path was `harness/specs/tasks/harness-gap-external-write-boundary.md`? Let me check: the S03 acceptance SHA256SUMS includes `harness/specs/tasks/harness-gap-external-write-boundary.md`. And the S03 doc/SHA256SUMS. Let me check the inventory S03 packet spec path.\n\nActually earlier the inventory showed S04's spec path as `harness/specs/tasks/browser-effect-provenance-boundary.md` with state `existing`, and testTargets `openclaw_runtime/src/agents/tools/agent-browser-tool.provenance.test.ts` state existing. The validator would require that test target file to exist. Let me check if that file exists... earlier I found `Test-Path openclaw_runtime/src/agents/tools/agent-browser-tool.provenance.test.ts` → False. So the B00 baseline validator already fails on S04's inventory entry regardless of my change. Let me verify by running the baseline check to see the pre-existing state.\n\nWait — but the check is `for (const test of packet.testTargets.filter state existing) readText(test.target)`. That would throw. But readText is wrapped in try/catch that pushes 'source/spec read failed'. Hmm, but the earlier S03 packet's testTargets might be `command` state.\n\nLet me just run the baseline validator to see whether it's already red before/after my change. Since it's read-only, I can run it.\n\nActually the validator is the B00 packet's own gate; my S04 change doesn't touch source-inventory.json. So its state is whatever it is. Let me run it to know.\n\nBut note: the task targetScope is only the 5 files. I shouldn't modify source-inventory.json. Let me just ch